<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> stores facts in tables and lets you declare rules about them. A shop can require every order to name an existing customer, every order line to name an existing product, and every quantity to be greater than zero. The database checks those rules when the data changes, whichever application makes the change.</p>
<p>Tables also give an application different ways to read the same facts. From its customers, orders and payments, the shop can ask what Mina bought, which products sold most, or which orders still need paying. It asks in <strong>SQL</strong>, the language used to define, read and change data in most relational databases.</p>
<p>The work starts with deciding what each fact means. A product’s current price and the price a customer agreed to pay look alike, but changing one must leave the other alone. Those decisions become part of the database’s structure, and later changes to that structure have to account for the data and applications already using it.</p>
<p>Every example here follows a small shop selling tea and tea things. Mina Shah, Tariq Aziz and Edda Berg are its customers. We start with the facts behind their orders, then follow how an application reads them, changes them and keeps them safe. PostgreSQL supplies the concrete examples.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="facts-and-rules">
  <h2 id="facts-and-rules">Tables, keys and rules</h2>
  <p>A <strong>table</strong> records one kind of fact. Each <strong>row</strong> is one instance of it; each named <strong>column</strong> holds one part. In <code>customers</code>, a row says that customer 1 is Mina Shah. In <code>orders</code>, a row says that order 103 belongs to customer 1 and is pending. The number connects the facts without copying Mina’s name into every order.</p>
  <p>A <strong>primary key</strong> is the column, or combination of columns, chosen to identify one row. The database requires its values to be present and unique within that table. Here <code>customer_id</code> identifies a customer, and <code>order_id</code> identifies an order.</p>
  <p>A <strong>foreign key</strong> requires a value to match a key in a referenced table. The shop makes every order’s <code>customer_id</code> required and declares it a foreign key to <code>customers</code>. Several orders may name customer 1, but an order naming customer 99 is refused while no such customer exists.</p>
  <p>Figure 1 shows the connections. <strong>PK</strong> marks a primary key and <strong>FK</strong> a foreign key. Follow order 103 to its customer and its two lines, then follow each line to its product.</p>
  <ShopSchema />
  <p>Order 103 is Mina’s: its customer number is 1. Its first line is one blue mug at 20.00; its second is two packs of green tea at 4.50 each. The two lines belong in <code>order_items</code>, where each row records one product on one order. Putting them in columns such as <code>product_1</code> and <code>product_2</code> would make the table’s shape depend on how many things a customer buys.</p>
  <p>A line number identifies a line only within its order. Line 1 occurs on orders 101, 102 and 103, so <code>order_items</code> uses <code>(order_id, line_no)</code> together as its primary key. A second line 1 on order 103 would repeat that key and be refused. Line 1 on a different order is a different key.</p>
  <p>The two price columns record different facts. <code>products.price</code> is the price offered now; <code>order_items.unit_price</code> is the price agreed for that line. Raising the mug’s current price later leaves Mina’s line at 20.00.</p>
  <p>The shop can also declare what counts as a valid quantity. This is the quantity column’s definition in <code>order_items</code>:</p>
  <Sql code={quantity} label="The quantity column definition, an excerpt from order_items" />
  <p><code>integer</code> allows whole numbers. <code>NOT NULL</code> requires a value, and <code>CHECK (quantity &gt; 0)</code> requires it to be positive. Together they allow 1 or 2 and refuse zero, a negative number or a missing quantity. A rule the database enforces this way is a <strong>constraint</strong>. An attempted change of Mina’s tea quantity from 2 to 0 fails and leaves it at 2.</p>
  <p>The tables, columns and rules together form the database’s <strong>schema</strong>. It says what a row stands for, how to identify it, which other facts it may refer to and which values are allowed. Choosing a schema is choosing those meanings. The database can enforce a declared connection or quantity rule; it cannot tell that a price meant “agreed then” when the application writes “offered now”.</p>
</section>

